exports[`the playbook for fourteen household profiles > A. The example plan 1`] = `
"traditional, retire 2039

## 2039 · You retire (You 55 · Spouse 53)
1. Live on about $76,500 a year plus healthcare, in today’s dollars.
   (This is the budget the results are based on. Raise it with inflation each year: every figure here is in today’s dollars. Spending more than this, especially in the first years or in a bad market, is what runs money out early.)
2. Buy your own health insurance until each of you turns 65, when Medicare takes over.
   (The plan budgets about $38,800 a year for it in 2039, in today’s dollars (it rises 1.5% a year faster than prices, so look at this again each year), at full price with no subsidy, on top of your regular spending.)
3. Each January, take money out of the 401(k)/IRA up to the top of the 10% tax bracket.
   (This money is taxed whenever it comes out. Taking some every year while your income is low means it is taxed at the lowest rate, instead of piling up and being taxed at a higher rate later. For 2026 the top of the bracket is about $57,000 of income for a married couple (the $32,200 standard deduction plus the $24,800 bracket), minus any other income you already expect that year, such as a pension, interest or the taxable part of Social Security. Look the numbers up each year; the IRS raises them with inflation. Start with your account, the older of you, because it opens up penalty-free first; then Spouse’s.)
4. Don’t spend it while you are both under 59½: move it straight into a Roth IRA and write down the year.
   (Ask the brokerage for a “Roth conversion”. Spending that money would cost a 10% penalty; converting it costs only the income tax, and after 5 years it can be spent with no tax or penalty at all.)
5. For everything else you need this year, take money in this order, moving to the next account only when the one before is empty:
   a. Cash.
   b. Brokerage. You pay tax only on the growth, at the low capital-gains rate.
   c. Roth: only what you put in yourself, and conversions that are 5 or more years old. The growth and newer conversions stay put.
   d. If those run out while you are under 59½, the 401(k)/IRA is next, then Roth growth, both with the 10% penalty. The results above count how often that happens. (The IRS has exceptions, such as the Rule of 55, that the plan doesn’t count on.)
   (Cash and brokerage cost the least tax to spend. Roth money grows tax-free, so it is worth the most if it is left alone the longest.)
6. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
7. Once a year, put every invested account back to 70% stocks / 25% bonds / 5% cash (cash stays cash).
   (The results assume this mix in every account, every year. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
8. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2039: spent $115K; $36K from cash; $85K from brokerage; $51K moved from 401(k)/IRA to Roth; $5K in taxes.

## 2044 · You turn 60 (You 60 · Spouse 58)
1. Your 401(k)/IRA and all of your Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year you turn 60.)
2. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, your 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2044: spent $118K; $68K from brokerage; $56K from 401(k)/IRA; $5K in taxes.

## 2046 · Spouse turns 60 (You 62 · Spouse 60)
1. Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA and all of Spouse’s Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year Spouse turns 60.)
2. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2046: spent $120K; $137K from 401(k)/IRA; $18K in taxes.

## 2049 · You turn 65 (You 65 · Spouse 63)
1. You move to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for you goes from about $22,500 to about $10,600 a year in 2049, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2049: spent $110K; $125K from 401(k)/IRA; $15K in taxes.

## 2051 · You start Social Security (age 67) · Spouse turns 65 (You 67 · Spouse 65)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before you turn 67.
   (From then on your benefit adds about $22K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
2. Spouse moves to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for Spouse goes from about $23,200 to about $10,900 a year in 2051, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2051: spent $98K; $11K from Social Security; $99K from 401(k)/IRA; $11K in taxes.

## 2053 · Spouse starts Social Security (age 67) (You 69 · Spouse 67)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before Spouse turns 67.
   (From then on Spouse’s benefit adds about $22K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2053: spent $99K; $33K from Social Security; $76K from 401(k)/IRA; $10K in taxes.

## 2059 · You turn 75 (You 75 · Spouse 73)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from your 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2059: spent $101K; $43K from Social Security; $67K from 401(k)/IRA; $3K of that required by the IRS; $9K in taxes.

## 2061 · Spouse turns 75 (You 77 · Spouse 75)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2061: spent $102K; $43K from Social Security; $69K from 401(k)/IRA; $57K of that required by the IRS; $9K in taxes.
"
`;

exports[`the playbook for fourteen household profiles > B. Modest income, small balances, retiring at 66 and 64 1`] = `
"traditional, retire 2050

## 2050 · You retire (You 66 · Spouse 64)
1. Live on about $51,000 a year plus healthcare, in today’s dollars.
   (This is the budget the results are based on. Raise it with inflation each year: every figure here is in today’s dollars. Spending more than this, especially in the first years or in a bad market, is what runs money out early.)
2. Buy your own health insurance until Spouse turns 65, when Medicare takes over.
   (The plan budgets about $22,900 a year for it in 2050, in today’s dollars (it rises 1.5% a year faster than prices, so look at this again each year), at full price with no subsidy, on top of your regular spending.)
3. Each January, take money out of the 401(k)/IRA up to the top of the 10% tax bracket.
   (This money is taxed whenever it comes out. Taking some every year while your income is low means it is taxed at the lowest rate, instead of piling up and being taxed at a higher rate later. For 2026 the top of the bracket is about $57,000 of income for a married couple (the $32,200 standard deduction plus the $24,800 bracket), minus any other income you already expect that year, such as a pension, interest or the taxable part of Social Security. Look the numbers up each year; the IRS raises them with inflation. Start with your account, the older of you, because it opens up penalty-free first; then Spouse’s.)
4. Spend what you need from it and move whatever is left over into a Roth IRA.
   (A “Roth conversion”: the money grows tax-free from then on, and it shrinks the withdrawals the IRS will require later.)
5. For everything else you need this year, take money in this order, moving to the next account only when the one before is empty:
   a. Cash.
   b. Brokerage. You pay tax only on the growth, at the low capital-gains rate.
   c. The 401(k)/IRA, beyond the yearly amount above.
   d. Roth.
   (Cash and brokerage cost the least tax to spend. Roth money grows tax-free, so it is worth the most if it is left alone the longest.)
6. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
7. Once a year, put every invested account back to 70% stocks / 25% bonds / 5% cash (cash stays cash).
   (The results assume this mix in every account, every year. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
8. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2050: spent $85K; $19K from cash; $15K from brokerage; $54K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2051 · You start Social Security (age 67) · Spouse turns 65 (You 67 · Spouse 65)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before you turn 67.
   (From then on your benefit adds about $18K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
2. Spouse moves to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for Spouse goes from about $23,200 to about $10,900 a year in 2051, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2051: spent $73K; $9K from Social Security; $17K from brokerage; $50K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2053 · Spouse starts Social Security (age 67) (You 69 · Spouse 67)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before Spouse turns 67.
   (From then on Spouse’s benefit adds about $16K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
