exports[`the playbook for fourteen household profiles > F. Already 63 and 61, retiring now; one already collects Social Security; the trust-fund cut is ahead 1`] = `
"traditional, retire 2026

## 2026 · You retire (You 63 · Spouse 61)
1. Live on about $76,500 a year plus healthcare, in today’s dollars.
   (This is the budget the results are based on. Raise it with inflation each year: every figure here is in today’s dollars. Spending more than this, especially in the first years or in a bad market, is what runs money out early.)
2. Buy your own health insurance until each of you turns 65, when Medicare takes over.
   (The plan budgets about $32,000 a year for it in 2026, in today’s dollars (it rises 1.5% a year faster than prices, so look at this again each year), at full price with no subsidy, on top of your regular spending.)
3. Each January, take money out of the 401(k)/IRA up to the top of the 10% tax bracket.
   (This money is taxed whenever it comes out. Taking some every year while your income is low means it is taxed at the lowest rate, instead of piling up and being taxed at a higher rate later. For 2026 the top of the bracket is about $57,000 of income for a married couple (the $32,200 standard deduction plus the $24,800 bracket), minus any other income you already expect that year, such as a pension, interest or the taxable part of Social Security. Look the numbers up each year; the IRS raises them with inflation. Start with your account, the older of you, because it opens up penalty-free first; then Spouse’s.)
4. Spend what you need from it and move whatever is left over into a Roth IRA.
   (A “Roth conversion”: the money grows tax-free from then on, and it shrinks the withdrawals the IRS will require later.)
5. For everything else you need this year, take money in this order, moving to the next account only when the one before is empty:
   a. Cash.
   b. Brokerage. You pay tax only on the growth, at the low capital-gains rate.
   c. The 401(k)/IRA, beyond the yearly amount above.
   d. Roth.
   (Cash and brokerage cost the least tax to spend. Roth money grows tax-free, so it is worth the most if it is left alone the longest.)
6. You already collect Social Security.
   (It covers part of each year, and the accounts cover the rest.)
7. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
8. Once a year, put every invested account back to 70% stocks / 25% bonds / 5% cash (cash stays cash).
   (The results assume this mix in every account, every year. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
9. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2026: spent $109K; $21K from Social Security; $30K from cash; $22K from brokerage; $39K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2028 · You turn 65 (You 65 · Spouse 63)
1. You move to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for you goes from about $16,500 to about $7,700 a year in 2028, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2028: spent $101K; $21K from Social Security; $43K from brokerage; $41K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2030 · Spouse turns 65 (You 67 · Spouse 65)
1. Spouse moves to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for Spouse goes from about $17,000 to about $8,000 a year in 2030, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2030: spent $92K; $21K from Social Security; $34K from brokerage; $41K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2032 · Spouse starts Social Security (age 67) · Social Security cut (You 69 · Spouse 67)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before Spouse turns 67.
   (From then on Spouse’s benefit adds about $23K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 78% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
2. Expect smaller Social Security checks from 2032.
   (The plan counts only 78% of each check from then on, sliding to 62% by 2100, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in the program’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more than planned.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2032: spent $93K; $28K from Social Security; $33K from brokerage; $36K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2038 · You turn 75 (You 75 · Spouse 73)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from your 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2038: spent $94K; $39K from Social Security; $8K from brokerage; $55K from 401(k)/IRA; $55K of that required by the IRS; $7K in taxes.

## 2040 · Spouse turns 75 (You 77 · Spouse 75)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2040: spent $95K; $39K from Social Security; $89K from 401(k)/IRA; $89K of that required by the IRS; $20K not needed and reinvested; $13K in taxes.
"
`;

